1
00:00:17,331 --> 00:00:24,218
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is the Avijja Sutta, Sutta on Ignorance, Anguttara 1061.

2
00:00:27,141 --> 00:00:36,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it is said that no first point of ignorance is evident, before which there was no ignorance, and afterwards ignorance came to be.

3
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:45,474
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as you said, quite a lot, ignorance is beginningless.

4
00:00:46,594 --> 00:00:47,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Ignorance of what?

5
00:00:51,598 --> 00:00:58,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can answer in many different ways, but the point is, practically speaking, you don't start in a neutral position.

6
00:01:00,444 --> 00:01:08,770
[SPEAKER_00]: You already start, begin, hindered, obstructed, obsessed, limited, restricted,

7
00:01:10,851 --> 00:01:12,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what ignorance is.

8
00:01:16,595 --> 00:01:19,357
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you... why is it that...

9
00:01:20,678 --> 00:01:35,490
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, yeah, obviously, it's because that's how it is, but why is it so sort of difficult to not see the possibility of not ignoring?

10
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:40,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because you have no reference point that's not affected by ignorance.

11
00:01:41,405 --> 00:01:44,786
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any standpoint you take, partakes in ignorance.

12
00:01:44,826 --> 00:01:47,987
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in ignorance.

13
00:01:48,027 --> 00:01:49,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's so difficult.

14
00:01:49,368 --> 00:01:53,189
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no standpoint can just suddenly show you a situation.

15
00:01:53,869 --> 00:01:55,070
[SPEAKER_00]: There are no markers for it.

16
00:01:57,851 --> 00:02:00,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Any standpoint you take, will be ignorant.

17
00:02:01,332 --> 00:02:03,813
[SPEAKER_00]: So, top to bottom.

18
00:02:05,293 --> 00:02:06,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, what do you do?

19
00:02:06,214 --> 00:02:08,074
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you then figure your way out?

20
00:02:08,194 --> 00:02:10,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's where the gradual training comes in.

21
00:02:11,558 --> 00:02:22,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You figure it out by upholding certain behaviors, sense restraint, virtue, which in return diminishes.

22
00:02:23,224 --> 00:02:25,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that you can see it, not at a time.

23
00:02:25,686 --> 00:02:32,529
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see it later on, after which your efforts would have been verified, but initially you can't see.

24
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:41,274
[SPEAKER_00]: You can make some sense, you know, out of it, if you're authentic and intelligent and so on, but fundamentally it comes down to doing it,

25
00:02:41,855 --> 00:02:49,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of your standpoints, keeping the precepts, guarding the sense doors, watching your intentions, and so on.

26
00:02:50,860 --> 00:03:02,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And that diminishes that vicious... weakens that vicious cycle of ignorance feeding more ignorance, standpoints feeding more standpoints.

27
00:03:02,387 --> 00:03:10,852
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you stick to those training precepts, and tame your mind within that, then you get to see your situation from the inside.

28
00:03:14,035 --> 00:03:15,315
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's sotāpati, basically.

29
00:03:15,415 --> 00:03:19,316
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what we said in that... the seven... the relay chariot sutta.

30
00:03:20,916 --> 00:03:26,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the views will be purified, not because you figured it all out intellectually and made sense out of it.

31
00:03:26,438 --> 00:03:30,558
[SPEAKER_00]: The views are purified when your behavior is purified, when the composure is regained within that.

32
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:38,800
[SPEAKER_00]: So, samādhi results... direct result from nothing else other than sensory strength and precepts.

33
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:43,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Purification of the views is a result of that.

34
00:03:45,315 --> 00:03:46,976
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not natural.

35
00:03:47,396 --> 00:03:51,837
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not something that one would...

36
00:03:52,898 --> 00:03:57,899
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, yeah, what is natural is to want pleasure.

37
00:03:57,939 --> 00:03:58,800
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

38
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:03,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, the most natural thing is to put your standpoints first.

39
00:04:04,361 --> 00:04:04,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

40
00:04:04,982 --> 00:04:06,002
[SPEAKER_00]: The craving for the views.

41
00:04:07,102 --> 00:04:08,843
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the views are the most blameworthy thing.

42
00:04:08,863 --> 00:04:12,864
[SPEAKER_00]: The wrong view is the most blameworthy thing, as the Buddha said.

43
00:04:13,004 --> 00:04:14,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you would go against that grain.

44
00:04:15,328 --> 00:04:16,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Against that tendency.

45
00:04:16,209 --> 00:04:21,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that you can stop having views, but you can certainly stop emphasizing them.

46
00:04:22,272 --> 00:04:31,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So, put your views aside, review your behavior, your actions, and purify it like... like a skilled goldsmith would be purifying gold.

47
00:04:31,937 --> 00:04:36,820
[SPEAKER_00]: First from coarse impurities, then more refined impurities, then more refined impurities.

48
00:04:38,721 --> 00:04:42,883
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, next thing you know, you don't have those standpoints anymore.

49
00:04:44,016 --> 00:04:45,817
[SPEAKER_02]: You can see what proceeds at the standpoint.

50
00:04:46,457 --> 00:04:47,878
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you just don't have them anymore.

51
00:04:47,898 --> 00:04:49,859
[SPEAKER_02]: They're gone, because they have been unplugged.

52
00:04:50,419 --> 00:04:54,721
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you've seen the context, or you've been able to... No, because you purified them.

53
00:04:55,662 --> 00:05:03,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You purified them through sense restraint, watchfulness of your intentions, general taming of your mind.

54
00:05:06,067 --> 00:05:11,069
[SPEAKER_00]: And then these views have lost their power, their weight.

55
00:05:13,392 --> 00:05:18,653
[SPEAKER_00]: The standpoints, yeah, the wrong emphasis is disappeared.

56
00:05:20,834 --> 00:05:25,895
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can have, still, thoughts and standpoints, but they won't be carrying the same weight.

57
00:05:26,635 --> 00:05:36,537
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't be emphasized, because now you know exactly how the mind is stained, how the views are diminished, and how the freedom from suffering is developed.

58
00:05:39,697 --> 00:05:42,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Through gradual training, not through the opinions on it.

59
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:50,802
[SPEAKER_00]: And the opinions that persist would then just be, without that weight, would be a correct representation of what you did.

60
00:05:50,822 --> 00:05:51,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the right view.

61
00:05:55,663 --> 00:06:03,125
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, an arahant cannot ignore... No, no, no, because arahant's mind cannot be untamed anymore.

62
00:06:04,725 --> 00:06:07,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot be provoked anymore, cannot be disturbed, cannot be perturbed.

63
00:06:08,626 --> 00:06:11,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, no views, no standpoints can arise.

64
00:06:13,797 --> 00:06:20,321
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this sutta gives ten fuels for the... for ignorance.

65
00:06:20,341 --> 00:06:21,281
[SPEAKER_00]: For ignorance.

66
00:06:22,282 --> 00:06:23,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just to clarify before going there.

67
00:06:24,283 --> 00:06:26,484
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ignorance is not on the level of the views.

68
00:06:28,945 --> 00:06:32,187
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not on the level of, oh, I haven't read enough suttas to figure things out.

69
00:06:32,627 --> 00:06:39,171
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of that self-induced delusion, distraction, greed, aversion through your acts and your choices.

70
00:06:40,565 --> 00:06:41,746
[SPEAKER_00]: That is what ignorance is.

71
00:06:42,006 --> 00:06:43,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Choosing to turn a blind eye.

72
00:06:43,928 --> 00:06:46,450
[SPEAKER_02]: That's basically what the Sutta says, yeah.

73
00:06:46,710 --> 00:06:48,212
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

74
00:06:51,294 --> 00:06:56,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, it is evident that there is a specific condition for ignorance.

75
00:06:56,579 --> 00:07:04,726
[SPEAKER_02]: I say that ignorance is fueled by the five hindrances.

76
00:07:05,507 --> 00:07:08,269
[SPEAKER_02]: As you say, yeah, it's acting out of

77
00:07:08,942 --> 00:07:16,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Acting out of pressures, obsessions, I mean, the five hindrances, they all boil down to greed, aversion, distraction, one way or the other.

78
00:07:18,390 --> 00:07:21,672
[SPEAKER_00]: So, choosing to act out of those things, you're fueling a vision.

79
00:07:21,972 --> 00:07:27,517
[SPEAKER_00]: You're fueling the attitude of ignoring, turning a blind eye, of not being clear.

80
00:07:31,307 --> 00:07:34,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the translation of moha is delusion.

81
00:07:34,089 --> 00:07:34,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusion, yeah.

82
00:07:34,889 --> 00:07:35,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Moha.

83
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:37,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Sounds... sounds like delusion.

84
00:07:37,811 --> 00:07:38,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

85
00:07:38,312 --> 00:07:39,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Like moha.

86
00:07:40,573 --> 00:07:41,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You're a fool, kind of.

87
00:07:41,914 --> 00:07:44,376
[SPEAKER_01]: But I like the the translation of distraction.

88
00:07:44,596 --> 00:07:45,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

89
00:07:45,196 --> 00:07:46,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's a practical thing.

90
00:07:46,517 --> 00:07:48,418
[SPEAKER_01]: You know when you're distracting yourself.

91
00:07:49,379 --> 00:07:50,620
[SPEAKER_01]: There's some recognition there.

92
00:07:52,361 --> 00:07:58,705
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, I guess, so most person who's ignorant, he's distracted.

93
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And he's ignorant because he keeps himself distracted.

94
00:08:03,555 --> 00:08:03,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

95
00:08:06,527 --> 00:08:09,089
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, he doesn't know that he's necessarily distracted.

96
00:08:09,569 --> 00:08:11,010
[SPEAKER_01]: He's engaged with the world.

97
00:08:11,270 --> 00:08:11,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

98
00:08:11,550 --> 00:08:15,233
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, an intoxicated person is not aware of his... that he's drunk.

99
00:08:15,613 --> 00:08:17,374
[SPEAKER_03]: I was just going to... Until he sobers up.

100
00:08:17,614 --> 00:08:25,920
[SPEAKER_03]: I was just going to bring up the intoxication, like, even the way that the Buddha talks about, like, the intoxication of youth, the intoxication of life.

101
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:26,980
[SPEAKER_03]: And health.

102
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:32,624
[SPEAKER_03]: And health, in the sense that, like, old age, sickness and death are not things that are hidden.

103
00:08:33,145 --> 00:08:36,567
[SPEAKER_03]: From people, things that are... they're very obvious.

104
00:08:36,687 --> 00:08:38,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor are the things that will come later.

105
00:08:38,569 --> 00:08:39,389
[SPEAKER_00]: They're already here.

106
00:08:39,529 --> 00:08:39,950
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

107
00:08:41,251 --> 00:09:00,704
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're already subjected... subject to the possibility of people all around you can fall... fall sick, die, get old, and so you... you could pay more heed to them, but it's like this... yeah, that feeling... intoxicates it.

108
00:09:00,804 --> 00:09:01,845
[SPEAKER_00]: An intoxication

109
00:09:02,645 --> 00:09:03,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot exist on its own.

110
00:09:04,146 --> 00:09:06,668
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you would sober up if you stop drinking.

111
00:09:06,869 --> 00:09:07,069
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

112
00:09:08,050 --> 00:09:13,274
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the fact that you're not sobering up means because you're still consuming alcohol.

113
00:09:14,515 --> 00:09:21,141
[SPEAKER_00]: In this sense, the fact that you're not sobering up from these intoxications is because you're still consuming things that keep you intoxicated.

114
00:09:22,362 --> 00:09:28,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual desire, acting out of aversion and distraction.

115
00:09:28,327 --> 00:09:29,868
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like a similar principle of

116
00:09:31,617 --> 00:09:36,198
[SPEAKER_03]: Trying, basically, forgetfulness, almost, right?

117
00:09:36,478 --> 00:09:42,019
[SPEAKER_03]: Deliberately induced forgetfulness of the things that are obvious.

118
00:09:42,179 --> 00:09:45,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because it's like a coping habit.

119
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:56,222
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's funny, because you only need to try and forget that thing, if it's already kind of, on some level, recognized that it's a problem.

120
00:09:56,282 --> 00:09:57,882
[SPEAKER_03]: Otherwise, you wouldn't need to

121
00:09:58,662 --> 00:10:02,524
[SPEAKER_01]: But the forgetfulness is a pleasure.

122
00:10:02,544 --> 00:10:03,324
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a safety.

123
00:10:04,265 --> 00:10:07,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Even though you know what you're forgetting about.

124
00:10:09,087 --> 00:10:14,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Just that, you know, shutting your eyes, covering your head is pleasant.

125
00:10:18,791 --> 00:10:20,292
[SPEAKER_03]: But if... that's the funny thing.

126
00:10:20,312 --> 00:10:20,992
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if you...

127
00:10:22,927 --> 00:10:38,031
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if you are, say, even in terms of intoxication, like, if you're in a situation where you know it's really, you know, you must not forget, you must not become just careless or something.

128
00:10:38,071 --> 00:10:40,371
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know, you're driving or something or whatever.

129
00:10:41,031 --> 00:10:43,272
[SPEAKER_03]: Most people, at least, if you have some degree of

130
00:10:45,254 --> 00:10:59,801
[SPEAKER_03]: of carefulness or whatever, you wouldn't, you wouldn't, you know, if you might, I don't know, drink a small amount of alcohol or something, but if you were to realize, oh, I'm actually, I'm starting to become intoxicated, it would be

131
00:11:01,014 --> 00:11:01,975
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not seen as dangerous.

132
00:11:01,995 --> 00:11:08,378
[SPEAKER_03]: It would be seen as, okay, I can't let myself forget this, because I need, because I can see the danger of forgetting.

133
00:11:09,419 --> 00:11:19,825
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, in other words, you can only, you know, take pleasure in forgetting if you're already intoxicated to some degree.

134
00:11:20,085 --> 00:11:21,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and that's that's exactly the point.

135
00:11:24,572 --> 00:11:26,553
[SPEAKER_00]: You already start as being intoxicated.

136
00:11:27,213 --> 00:11:29,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's no reference point of sobriety.

137
00:11:30,114 --> 00:11:31,975
[SPEAKER_02]: The neutral position is seeing the danger.

138
00:11:32,995 --> 00:11:34,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's how you work towards the neutral position.

139
00:11:34,936 --> 00:11:42,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yes, you could still technically become careless, but then the danger is much more apparent in doing so.

140
00:11:42,419 --> 00:11:46,280
[SPEAKER_00]: As opposed to somebody who is in the beginning, when there's like no reference point for the danger.

141
00:11:48,081 --> 00:11:53,343
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, like, that's why, you know, I know when I started reading the suttas, you know, back as a layman and

142
00:11:54,997 --> 00:12:07,481
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, you know, you have all these views and so on, but I remember, like, you read the suttas and it just seemed almost as if a matter of a fact that monks would just get the right view upon going into seclusion.

143
00:12:07,841 --> 00:12:10,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there is the Buddha, then they go and dwell diligent and withdrawn.

144
00:12:12,105 --> 00:12:24,855
[SPEAKER_00]: But there was kind of not quite fitting with the assumption that the right view is on the level of figuring out things and having this magical kind of breakthrough insight type into stuff that just suddenly surprised you.

145
00:12:25,996 --> 00:12:28,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it was like, what are they diligent about?

146
00:12:29,999 --> 00:12:31,080
[SPEAKER_00]: What are they withdrawn from?

147
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:32,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Where do you like... Oh!

148
00:12:32,501 --> 00:12:38,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody makes the effort, right view was the necessary result of it.

149
00:12:39,106 --> 00:12:40,607
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody makes that right effort,

150
00:12:42,868 --> 00:12:56,178
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, just, he comes, he talks to the Buddha, the Buddha instructs him, he goes, and he dwells resolute and diligent, and then, in no time, and then another one, and then another one, and then another one, and then you may think, oh, well, because, you know, they were lucky, talking to the Buddha and so on, but no.

151
00:12:56,518 --> 00:12:58,400
[SPEAKER_00]: They were the ones, diligent and resolute.

152
00:12:58,540 --> 00:13:00,861
[SPEAKER_00]: So, diligent and resolute about what?

153
00:13:00,961 --> 00:13:05,845
[SPEAKER_00]: About spinning their mind constantly around how to figure this out, how to figure this out, rehashing everything.

154
00:13:08,218 --> 00:13:26,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, maybe some, to some extent, but there is probably some more kind of practical unifying thread, that all these different monks, regardless, you know, one might have arrived through faith, another might have arrived through discernment and reasoning, another one might have arrived through composure.

155
00:13:26,584 --> 00:13:36,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, they all simply partake in diligence and resoluteness of keeping the mind away from unwholesome states, and they all arrive at the right view and arahantship.

156
00:13:39,008 --> 00:13:42,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize, aha, because that's where the emphasis is.

157
00:13:42,629 --> 00:13:48,391
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not on a magical breakthrough through sensation experience of a meditation.

158
00:13:48,911 --> 00:13:56,714
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not certainly through scholastic figuring out, you know, the implicit meaning, the hidden meanings of the Pali terms.

159
00:13:57,334 --> 00:14:01,175
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also not on the level of account... on the kind of just mere reasoning.

160
00:14:01,836 --> 00:14:05,517
[SPEAKER_00]: You can reason all you want, but as I said many times before,

161
00:14:06,406 --> 00:14:09,962
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind is not tamed through the gradual training in the right order,

162
00:14:10,476 --> 00:14:11,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Obedrawal and diligence.

163
00:14:12,357 --> 00:14:21,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, diligence not, I'm diligent, I'm gonna walk 20 kilometers and carry 50 liters of water on my back and live on top of a mountain and I'm diligent, I'm diligent.

164
00:14:22,062 --> 00:14:26,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, that's one way of being diligent, but that's not the diligence we talk about.

165
00:14:26,704 --> 00:14:38,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Diligence that matters is diligence of recognizing the unwholesome intention whenever it arises on a day-to-day basis and simply not acting out of it, enduring it without losing context in regard to it.

166
00:14:40,230 --> 00:14:42,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Every single time, no exception.

167
00:14:43,151 --> 00:14:44,432
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all.

168
00:14:44,452 --> 00:14:46,493
[SPEAKER_00]: That's literally the all that you need to do.

169
00:14:49,634 --> 00:14:55,856
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, whether you reason, whether you ponder, whether you speculate, that will all sort of fall into place.

170
00:15:06,161 --> 00:15:09,022
[SPEAKER_02]: I say that the five hindrances are fueled by

171
00:15:09,740 --> 00:15:11,341
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why can't I stop acting out?

172
00:15:12,121 --> 00:15:15,842
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm sure everybody at some point has asked himself that question.

173
00:15:39,145 --> 00:15:39,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Here is why.

174
00:15:40,706 --> 00:15:41,686
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're not restrained.

175
00:15:42,066 --> 00:15:42,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Not restrained enough.

176
00:15:44,567 --> 00:15:55,271
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still, willingly, consciously, in full volition, choosing non-sense restraint, when those options present themselves.

177
00:15:56,351 --> 00:16:05,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, maybe not as bad as you used to be or something, but factually, if you still can't help but act out, it's because you still have not purified the restraint.

178
00:16:10,246 --> 00:16:11,206
[SPEAKER_00]: The relay chariots.

179
00:16:15,608 --> 00:16:20,170
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing one's responsibility here.

180
00:16:20,230 --> 00:16:20,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

181
00:16:20,710 --> 00:16:35,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing that the direct reason for you still acting out is your choices made on a day-to-day basis that contradict the sensory strength.

182
00:16:35,916 --> 00:16:38,497
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're still fueling it and then it burns.

183
00:16:40,016 --> 00:16:43,378
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be surprised all you want, but you are the one pouring the fuel.

184
00:16:57,426 --> 00:17:04,550
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I say that the lack of sense-restraint is fueled by a lack of mindfulness and situational awareness.

185
00:17:05,410 --> 00:17:07,831
[SPEAKER_02]: So,

186
00:17:11,545 --> 00:17:14,087
[SPEAKER_02]: Like you're saying, this contradiction.

187
00:17:14,807 --> 00:17:17,449
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you're acting out of...

188
00:17:18,189 --> 00:17:21,611
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you lose sense-restraint, because you lost the context.

189
00:17:21,812 --> 00:17:23,333
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how'd you lose the context, then?

190
00:17:24,073 --> 00:17:25,114
[SPEAKER_00]: How'd you lose mindfulness?

191
00:17:26,955 --> 00:17:30,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that happened accidentally, just like, you blink and it's gone?

192
00:17:32,578 --> 00:17:39,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, again, by choosing to repeatedly attend, intend, engage with,

193
00:17:40,175 --> 00:17:44,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that you know eventually lead to unwholesome.

194
00:17:46,258 --> 00:17:55,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they might not be bad at its face value right there, obviously bad, but you know like, yeah, but this will lead to what it led so many times before.

195
00:17:55,322 --> 00:17:58,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And you ignore that hint, you ignore that sign, that cue.

196
00:17:59,703 --> 00:18:02,025
[SPEAKER_03]: Not guarding the sense doors, basically.

197
00:18:02,065 --> 00:18:02,265
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

198
00:18:03,185 --> 00:18:09,808
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when it comes to breaking the sense restraint, it's like the most natural decision at the time, because the mind is already inclined in that direction way before.

199
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:12,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, you can't stop that momentum.

200
00:18:13,381 --> 00:18:31,526
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, if one practices sense restraint, then the possibility of seeing, yeah, this context of the already arisen nature of experience proceeding, yeah, my self or, yeah, that is possible.

201
00:18:31,686 --> 00:18:35,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, that remains, yeah, that remains unobstructed.

202
00:18:35,407 --> 00:18:37,107
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

203
00:18:37,127 --> 00:18:39,648
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always there, but the possibility of seeing it is...

204
00:18:40,706 --> 00:18:59,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing, like, while the views are still emphasized, a person might be making loads of effort, like, tremendous amount of effort to keep clarifying that, keep clarifying what's already there, what isn't before me, not mine, but not actually purifying the sense restraint, withdrawal and not acting out of intentions.

205
00:19:00,544 --> 00:19:03,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're still... your effort's wasted, in a sense.

206
00:19:04,780 --> 00:19:09,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your emphasis is in the end, not the beginning of it, of that development.

207
00:19:09,901 --> 00:19:10,581
[SPEAKER_00]: The first try.

208
00:19:10,941 --> 00:19:11,121
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

209
00:19:12,022 --> 00:19:21,664
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, sure, bring back that, oh, I remind myself of the context, but then also continue and ask yourself, why did you lose the context in the first place?

210
00:19:21,824 --> 00:19:23,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you need to keep reminding yourself?

211
00:19:23,444 --> 00:19:26,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you need to keep rehashing things that you understood?

212
00:19:27,225 --> 00:19:28,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are they still liable to doubt?

213
00:19:29,086 --> 00:19:30,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you bothered by it?

214
00:19:30,868 --> 00:19:37,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, well, because the taming that precedes it has not been developed, not sufficiently developed.

215
00:19:37,171 --> 00:19:38,372
[SPEAKER_00]: So, of course, it's not going to be perfect.

216
00:19:38,392 --> 00:19:41,474
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you're suddenly going to stop everything else and you do one, two, three.

217
00:19:41,594 --> 00:19:43,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, technically, theoretically possible.

218
00:19:44,295 --> 00:20:00,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind will still be jumping at the end, in the beginning, the middle and so on, but as long as you now know what comes first is first, you will not get, like, too carried away, say, in spinning, you know, doubt and trying to figure it out,

219
00:20:00,885 --> 00:20:12,075
[SPEAKER_00]: While you know that, well, this is the result, this is the symptom of an underlying problem of insufficient, enduring things on the right level, insufficient restraint.

220
00:20:14,017 --> 00:20:26,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say insufficient restraint, it does not mean, okay, so now I will really restrain myself, I will lock myself in a little box, I won't move, I will barely breathe, I will not do anything, because that's not sense restraint, that's now self-torture.

221
00:20:27,677 --> 00:20:44,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense-restraint is when you are moving around freely throughout your day and then you have an intention that's directed towards sense-desire, aversion, distraction on the level of the body, speech or mind.

222
00:20:45,072 --> 00:20:47,214
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's which you don't act.

223
00:20:47,294 --> 00:20:48,095
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you endure.

224
00:20:49,636 --> 00:20:54,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you say that you

225
00:20:56,745 --> 00:20:58,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Know that that's what you're supposed to do.

226
00:21:00,008 --> 00:21:00,969
[SPEAKER_01]: You know enough of that.

227
00:21:01,809 --> 00:21:07,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Since, you know, indulgence is wrong, it's going to block my progress, everything.

228
00:21:08,655 --> 00:21:09,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm restrained.

229
00:21:09,836 --> 00:21:11,658
[SPEAKER_01]: I keep precepts.

230
00:21:15,181 --> 00:21:18,564
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the pressure comes to break those precepts, or break restraint.

231
00:21:20,466 --> 00:21:22,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And all the knowledge you have,

232
00:21:25,265 --> 00:21:25,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't work.

233
00:21:25,986 --> 00:21:27,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's too distant.

234
00:21:28,110 --> 00:21:28,931
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very nice.

235
00:21:29,212 --> 00:21:30,755
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right.

236
00:21:30,875 --> 00:21:36,404
[SPEAKER_01]: It's deep and meaningful, whatever, but it's too far away.

237
00:21:39,607 --> 00:21:42,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And you break your restraint, to whatever degree.

238
00:21:43,448 --> 00:21:45,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And you... ah, okay, ah, yeah.

239
00:21:46,029 --> 00:21:47,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And you suffer on account of that.

240
00:21:48,669 --> 00:21:49,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you try again.

241
00:21:49,970 --> 00:21:50,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

242
00:21:50,390 --> 00:21:51,490
[SPEAKER_01]: To build up the restraint.

243
00:21:52,350 --> 00:21:53,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Build up the restraint.

244
00:21:53,611 --> 00:21:58,832
[SPEAKER_01]: And listen, you know, hear the same discussion on sense-restraint again and again.

245
00:21:58,952 --> 00:22:00,052
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the pressure builds up.

246
00:22:01,312 --> 00:22:05,753
[SPEAKER_01]: And once again, you... With all that knowledge, you break that sense-restraint.

247
00:22:05,954 --> 00:22:06,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

248
00:22:06,234 --> 00:22:07,594
[SPEAKER_02]: So, so what...

249
00:22:09,126 --> 00:22:14,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's to say, that's a person who is just alone, doing the practice themselves.

250
00:22:18,451 --> 00:22:24,374
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's... you can get stuck in a loop by yourself.

251
00:22:25,314 --> 00:22:30,196
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you got all the information about the dangers of sensual indulgence.

252
00:22:31,197 --> 00:22:36,840
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to keep sensual strength, yet you yourself are breaking it.

253
00:22:38,174 --> 00:22:41,236
[SPEAKER_01]: And then trying again and breaking it, so you just insulate it.

254
00:22:42,477 --> 00:22:43,297
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the thing.

255
00:22:43,417 --> 00:23:01,789
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, when it comes to that, when your inspiration and things you know kind of drifts away into distance, and now pressure to break sensory strain is there, and you act out of it, well, see, the point is that that pressure to break the sensory strain does not just magically appear out of nowhere.

256
00:23:03,055 --> 00:23:04,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Does not... does not come overnight.

257
00:23:05,035 --> 00:23:13,758
[SPEAKER_00]: You were just like one moment, you were... you were really like full-on established in the right context, everything is clear, and the next moment it's... oh, you're about to act out.

258
00:23:13,818 --> 00:23:17,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, all of it is gone, suddenly, hundred miles away.

259
00:23:17,338 --> 00:23:17,478
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

260
00:23:18,859 --> 00:23:18,999
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

261
00:23:19,179 --> 00:23:29,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there have been things in between that that person was careless about, that that person was negligent, distract himself with.

262
00:23:30,262 --> 00:23:31,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it was accumulating.

263
00:23:32,309 --> 00:23:34,370
[SPEAKER_01]: A kind of gradual training in the opposite.

264
00:23:34,390 --> 00:23:42,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so it was exactly, yeah, yeah, of course, whatever mind frequently thinks and ponders, that's what it will incline towards, as the suttas would say.

265
00:23:42,316 --> 00:23:57,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might not be thinking core stuff, but there is stuff on the level of distraction, indolence, oblivion, and so on, that you are repeatedly, repeatedly engaging, even in solitude and so on, and then the pressure is building up, and the clarity is drifting away.

266
00:23:59,370 --> 00:24:02,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I said before, like, it's recognizing the little things.

267
00:24:04,334 --> 00:24:07,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, you know, the Buddha said it himself, like the skillful cook and so on.

268
00:24:08,096 --> 00:24:19,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, before these states of mind come and pressure you, where you kind of know you're going to lose sense restraint to a degree, you start learning and foreseeing, like, the little telltales.

269
00:24:21,135 --> 00:24:22,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Danger is in the slightest fault.

270
00:24:22,375 --> 00:24:23,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, yeah.

271
00:24:23,196 --> 00:24:24,976
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is not a fault, technically.

272
00:24:25,616 --> 00:24:38,320
[SPEAKER_00]: But, every single time in the past, when I went and was engaging with things of this nature, here and there, I did have sensual arise later on.

273
00:24:38,641 --> 00:24:42,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Unrelated to this directly, but still related to the acts around it.

274
00:24:43,522 --> 00:24:44,862
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's when you build it up.

275
00:24:44,922 --> 00:24:45,123
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

276
00:24:46,987 --> 00:24:53,468
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that kind of... that pressure that has been accumulated and now it's making you act.

277
00:24:54,549 --> 00:24:55,889
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't come out of nowhere.

278
00:24:56,329 --> 00:24:56,929
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

279
00:25:00,130 --> 00:25:03,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you might seem that way, because for the same reason as we said when we started this.

280
00:25:03,890 --> 00:25:05,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're still emphasizing the end.

281
00:25:06,371 --> 00:25:10,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to understand that what you're going through is still the result of other actions that you've been ignoring.

282
00:25:11,372 --> 00:25:12,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot be otherwise.

283
00:25:12,912 --> 00:25:15,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes you can feel like, but look, I'm doing everything right.

284
00:25:16,735 --> 00:25:18,196
[SPEAKER_00]: And now there is this pressure here.

285
00:25:18,577 --> 00:25:19,538
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be impossible.

286
00:25:19,858 --> 00:25:20,779
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be impossible.

287
00:25:21,319 --> 00:25:26,684
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in the sutta, there's an analogy which is like, yeah, perfect for describing what you just said.

